import System.IO.Console;
program StaticAllocInitBad;

// A STATIC FIELD WHOSE INITIALIZER MUST ALLOCATE CANNOT HAVE ONE, and saying so is the whole point.
//
// A global exists before the program starts, so its value has to be a constant the compiler can
// write into the binary. A `String` and a `new T[n]()` are heap objects: there is nothing to write.
// The guard that refuses an unevaluable initializer asked `isNumericStaticType` first, so it covered
// the numeric half and let this half through -- no error, no warning, and the field quietly zeroed
// to a null pointer.
//
// What that cost, twice, on the day it was found: `static mutable long[] stageMs = new long[9]()`
// came out with length ZERO and failed later and elsewhere as a bounds panic inside an unrelated
// method; `static mutable String tag = "hello"` compiled clean and took the process down with an
// access violation on first read. Neither failure points anywhere near the line that caused it.
//
// The standard library's own `Test` class was written this way too and only survived because its
// runner calls `reset()` before every test. It uses `onClassLoad` now, which is the answer this
// error names.
public bundle Probe {
    public namespace App {

        public class Table {
            public static mutable String tag = "hello";
        }

        public class Main {
            public static method main(string[] args) returns void {
                System.IO.Console.println(Table.tag);
                return;
            }
        }
    }
}
